  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>The Atabo red pike cichlid, Lugubria lugubris, is a large pike cichlid from rivers in Suriname, Guyana and Brazil. Colourful when small, it quickly grows into an aggressive adult of up to 40 cm and will eat any fish small enough to be considered food. Pairs are difficult to form because males become increasingly aggressive as they mature, and prospective mates are often killed.</p><ul><li>Needs 27 °C or warmer</li><li>Needs a very large tank</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/crenicichla_lugubris_orinoco_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Orinoco population of Crenicichla lugubris has a lot of red in its coloration and can reach a length of around 50 cm, making it one of the largest species of the genus. It is suited to very large or public aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Has been recorded from the Rio Corantijn in Suriname, Rio Essequibo in Guyana, Rio Negro and Rio Uatumã in Brazil and Rio Branco in both Brazil and Guyana.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/crenicichla_lugubris_orinoco_en/",
      "html": "<p>Venezuela (Orinoco).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Well planted rivers and tributaries.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<ul><li>Standard length: 40 cm SL</li><li>Aquarium base: 180 × 60 cm</li><li>Volume: ~972 litres (180 × 60 × 90 cm)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Sand substrate is recommended. This species is likely to dig and arrange the decor to suit itself. Some rockwork should be provided to form caves as pikes like to have an area to which they can retreat. Plants should be robust, ideally potted or rooted to bogwood.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 27–29°C</li><li>pH: 5.5–6.5</li><li>Hardness: up to 15 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Feed a varied diet including a good quality cichlid pellet or flake, live or frozen foods such as bloodworm or brine shrimp, meaty foods such as prawn and mussel and vegetable matter in the form of spirulina or algae wafers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Can be aggressive, especially towards conspecifics. May be kept with other large cichlids or as a species only setup. Likely to eat any fish small enough to be considered food - and they have big mouths!</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/crenicichla_lugubris_orinoco_en/",
      "html": "<p>Juveniles show the typical \"French fries pattern\" and are schooling fish that feel very unsafe when kept alone. At puberty the fish see conspecifics only as competitors and try to bite and kill them whenever they have the opportunity. Once sexually mature, they form pairs that most often live in peace over long periods.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Male has white stripe on the dorsal fin. Females are more colourful.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>Very few reports of breeding in captivity. Believed to be a cave spawner. Excellent parental care is exhibited. Can be difficult to form a pair due to the increasing aggression from the male as the fish mature. Prospective mates are often killed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/lugubria-lugubris",
      "html": "<p>An attractive and colourful fish when small, this species quickly grows into a large aggressive adult, requiring a large tank.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/crenicichla_lugubris_orinoco_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish change their coloration drastically during their ontogenesis, and the older they become, the more beautiful they are. This population characteristically has many spots in the face, which can lead to confusion with C. lenticulata, which lives in the same area.</p><p>Lexicon: Crenicichla is ancient Greek, meaning \"Cichla with a comb\"; Cichla is another cichlid genus. lugubris is Latin, meaning \"the one that wears mourning\", referring to the coloration.</p>"
    }
  ],
